<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        x:Class="DicomRtNifti.App.Views.DicomToNiftiHelpWindow"
        Title="Help: DICOM → NIfTI" Width="780" Height="700"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner"
        CanResize="True" MinHeight="400" MinWidth="540"
        Background="{DynamicResource AppWindowBg}">
  <Window.Styles>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.h1">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="16"/>
      <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppAccentBrush}"/>
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,18,0,8"/>
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.h2">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13"/>
      <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppHeadingText}"/>
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,8,0,4"/>
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.body">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12"/>
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource AppBodyText}"/>
      <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap"/>
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,2,0,2"/>
    </Style>
  </Window.Styles>

  <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">

    <Border Grid.Row="0" Background="{DynamicResource AppAccentBrush}" Padding="16,10">
      <StackPanel>
        <TextBlock Text="Help: DICOM → NIfTI" FontSize="16" FontWeight="SemiBold" Foreground="{DynamicResource AppOnAccentText}"/>
        <TextBlock Text="How to convert DICOM RT-STRUCT, CT/MR/PT image series, and RT-DOSE into NIfTI files."
                   Foreground="{DynamicResource AppOnAccentSubtitle}" FontSize="11" Margin="0,4,0,0"/>
        <TextBlock Text="Research use only: this software is not a medical device and has not been cleared or approved by any regulatory body for clinical use."
                   Foreground="{DynamicResource AppOnAccentSubtitle}" FontSize="11" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,4,0,0"/>
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>

    <Border Grid.Row="1" Background="{DynamicResource AppSurface}" BorderBrush="{DynamicResource AppBorder}" BorderThickness="1"
            CornerRadius="4" Margin="12,12,12,0" Padding="20,12">
      <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
        <StackPanel>

          <TextBlock Classes="h1" Text="Quick start"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            For the common case (export every series for every selected patient, no anonymization,
            no resampling):
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">1.</Run> Set <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Input</Run> (the root of
            your DICOM archive — scanned recursively) and <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Output</Run>
            (where the NIfTI files land) via the <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Browse...</Run> buttons.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">2.</Run> Click <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Scan</Run>. The
            Patient / Study / Series tree fills in. Tick the patients (or individual
            series) you want to export. Click a series to see its geometry, links and tags in the
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Series inspector</Run> beside the tree; look at any row
            badged <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">non-uniform Z</Run> or
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">link guessed</Run> before exporting it.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">3.</Run> In the <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Data to export</Run>
            row of the bottom action bar, tick the artifact types you want:
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Export Images</Run> (CT/MR/PT →
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">image.nii.gz</Run>),
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Export Structures</Run> (RT-STRUCT → per-ROI masks under
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">masks/</Run>),
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Export Dose</Run> (RT-DOSE → one file per dose under
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">doses/{SeriesDescription}.nii.gz</Run>).
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">4.</Run> Click <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Convert Selected</Run>.
            A status bar shows progress; the log box below records each step.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">5.</Run> Outputs land under
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">{Output}/{PatientID}/{SeriesDate}_{SeriesDescription}/</Run>.
            A CSV manifest (<Run FontFamily="Consolas">export_manifest.csv</Run>) sits at the output root.
            See <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Output structure</Run> below for the full layout.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h1" Text="Workflow walkthroughs"/>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="A. Plain export of one cohort"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            The 5-step Quick start above. Defaults give you images + structures + dose for every
            selected series, ROIs keep their original DICOM names, original voxel spacing preserved,
            patient identifiers preserved in folder names.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="B. Anonymized batch for research"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Open <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Export Options</Run> (button in the bottom action bar) and
            tick <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Anonymize export</Run> before Convert Selected. Each
            exported identifier (MRN, Study UID, Series UID) is replaced by a deterministic per-identifier
            hash; the output layout uses the hashed identifiers in place of the patient / series folder
            names. An <Run FontFamily="Consolas">AnonymizationKey.json</Run> mapping each hash back to its
            original identifier is written beside the export, in
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">{Output}.key\</Run>, never inside it, so the export folder can be
            shared on its own. Re-exporting the same series in any later session reuses the previously
            assigned hash.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="C. Restricted ROI cohort with canonical renames"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Use this when different patients call the same structure different names (e.g.
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">PTV</Run>, <Run FontFamily="Consolas">PTV_5400</Run>,
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">Target</Run>) and you want one canonical mask per ROI.
            Open <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Export Options</Run> (button in the bottom action bar) —
            steps 1–3 below all live in that window.
            <LineBreak/>
            <LineBreak/>
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">1.</Run> Click <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Edit ROI Associations...</Run>
            and define alias sets, e.g. canonical
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">Target</Run> ↔ aliases
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">{ CTV, GTV, PTV }</Run>.
            <LineBreak/>
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">2.</Run> Tick <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Limit export to selected ROIs</Run>.
            <LineBreak/>
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">3.</Run> Click <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Select ROIs for Export...</Run>
            and tick the canonical names you want. Patients whose series contain none of the
            selected ROIs are auto-deselected in the tree.
            <LineBreak/>
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">4.</Run> Convert Selected. ROIs that match an alias are
            renamed to their canonical name on disk; unmatched ROIs are dropped.
            <LineBreak/>
            <LineBreak/>
            With <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Limit export to selected ROIs</Run> OFF but ROI Associations
            defined, matched ROIs are still renamed — but unmatched ones are also exported with
            their original names.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="D. Inventory pass (no .nii.gz files written)"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Set up the export options exactly as you would for a full export, then click
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Export Manifest Only</Run> instead of Convert Selected. The
            rasterizer runs against each selected RT-STRUCT to compute per-ROI volumes (in cc), and
            image spacings are read, but nothing is written except
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">export_manifest_meta.csv</Run> at the output root. Ideal for
            cohort sizing, ROI prevalence audits, and spacing surveys. The log carries the same
            slice-spacing warnings Convert Selected would: a series with mixed slice gaps is surveyed
            on the flattened grid a conversion would write, so its spacing columns and volumes carry
            the same error.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="E. Resampled to a target voxel spacing"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            In <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Export Options</Run> (bottom action bar), tick
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Resample to fixed spacing</Run>, click
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Set Spacing...</Run>, and enter X / Y / Z spacings in mm
            (e.g. 1.0 × 1.0 × 1.0). On Convert Selected, every output volume is resampled to that
            grid: image and dose with <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">linear</Run> interpolation, masks
            with <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">nearest-neighbour</Run>. The CSV manifest reflects the
            target spacing, not the source spacing.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h1" Text="Reference: top toolbar"/>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Input / Output"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Folder paths. <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Browse...</Run> opens a folder picker; you can
            also paste a path directly. <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Input</Run> is the root of the
            DICOM archive (scanned recursively — every file is opened). <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Output</Run>
            is where .nii.gz files and the CSV manifest are written; it is created if it does not exist.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Scan / Stop + progress"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Scan</Run> walks the Input folder in parallel, parses each
            DICOM file, and builds the Patient → Study → Series tree.
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Stop</Run> aborts an in-progress scan or conversion.
            The progress bar and adjacent status text show live percentages and per-step messages
            (e.g. "Rasterizing structures: PatientA/CT_HEAD"). The bigger log box at the bottom of
            the window accumulates a timestamped record of every action.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h1" Text="Reference: Patient / Study / Series tree"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Three-level hierarchy with a checkbox at the patient and the series level (studies are
            a structural grouping only). Each series row shows a modality badge in brackets:
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">[CT]</Run>, <Run FontFamily="Consolas">[MR]</Run>,
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">[PT]</Run>, <Run FontFamily="Consolas">[RTSTRUCT]</Run>,
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">[RTDOSE]</Run> (and any other modality found in the archive).
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            The <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Select all</Run> checkbox at the tree header toggles
            every patient at once. For each image series that has a linked RT-STRUCT, an
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">ROI Names</Run> expander appears underneath the row and lists
            every ROI name found in that structure set.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Two amber badges flag what the scan found that deserves a look, on the series row and,
            counted, on its patient row (so they show while the tree is collapsed):
            <LineBreak/>
            • <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">non-uniform Z</Run> — the series' slice gaps are mixed
              (say 3 mm through the target, 6 mm elsewhere). NIfTI stores one spacing per axis, so
              the export flattens them to one averaged spacing and every volume computed from it is
              off. The badge appears exactly when Convert Selected and Export Manifest Only would
              log the warning; hover it to read that warning.
            <LineBreak/>
            • <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">link guessed</Run> — an RT-STRUCT or RT-DOSE was attached to
              this series by the largest-series fallback: neither its referenced series UID nor its
              frame of reference matched an image series, so the series with the most slices was
              assumed.
            <LineBreak/>
            The scan log also counts the badged series. Clicking any series row shows it in the
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Series inspector</Run>.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h1" Text="Reference: Series inspector"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            The panel right of the tree describes the selected series from the headers the scan
            already read; nothing is converted. Drag the divider between the panels to resize them.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Geometry &amp; links"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            • <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Instances</Run> — files in the series.
            <LineBreak/>
            • <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Slice spacing</Run> — the median gap between consecutive
              slices. A reader that assumes a regular grid reports a different number on a mixed-gap
              series (first-to-last distance over slices − 1).
            <LineBreak/>
            • <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Distinct gaps</Run> — every different slice gap present. One
              value means a regular grid. Two values with no badge differ only by rounding (for
              example 0.62 / 0.63 mm on a 0.625 mm grid): flattening them moves a volume by under 5%.
            <LineBreak/>
            • <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Pixel spacing</Run> — in-plane row × column spacing in mm.
            <LineBreak/>
            • <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Z extent</Run> — first-to-last slice distance, with the z range.
            <LineBreak/>
            • <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Frame of reference</Run> and
              <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Series UID</Run> — selectable for copying.
            <LineBreak/>
            • <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">RTSTRUCT</Run> / <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">RTDOSE</Run> — how
              many structure sets and doses are linked to the series, and by which rule:
              <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">referenced series UID</Run> (the RT object names this series;
              authoritative), <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">frame of reference UID</Run> (it shares the
              series' frame of reference; ties go to the series with most slices), or
              <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">largest-series fallback</Run> (nothing matched; a guess).
            <LineBreak/>
            Amber notes repeat, word for word, the warnings a conversion of the series logs: mixed
            slice gaps, and slices skipped for lacking a usable ImagePositionPatient. A list of the
            linked RT objects follows, each with its ROI or file count and its link rule.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="DICOM tags"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            A read-only grid of curated attributes from the series' first slice and its first linked
            RT-STRUCT and RT-DOSE — the same lists, under the same names, that
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Select Metadata Tags...</Run> offers for the
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">metadata.json</Run> sidecar. Attributes absent from the file
            are left out. The files are read the first time the series is selected.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h1" Text="Reference: export options"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            The three <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Data to export</Run> checkboxes (Export Images /
            Export Structures / Export Dose) live in the bottom action bar, just above Convert Selected,
            alongside the Export Options button.
            Everything else — ROI filtering, output spacing, and anonymization — opens from the
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Export Options...</Run> button as a separate window you can leave
            open and reposition while you keep working in the main window. Its settings apply to Convert
            Selected and Export Manifest Only immediately, whether that window is open or closed.
            Every checkbox here and in the Data to export row is saved the moment you change it, so
            the next window opens with them as you left them.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Export Images / Export Structures / Export Dose"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Export Images</Run> writes the image series as
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">image.nii.gz</Run> per series (one file per series, not per
            slice).
            <LineBreak/>
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Export Structures</Run> rasterizes the linked RT-STRUCT into
            per-ROI binary masks under <Run FontFamily="Consolas">masks/{ROI_Name}.nii.gz</Run>.
            <LineBreak/>
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Export Dose</Run> writes the linked RT-DOSE as
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">doses/{SeriesDescription}.nii.gz</Run> (one file per dose,
            named for the RT-DOSE Series Description with filesystem-invalid characters replaced),
            applying any <Run FontFamily="Consolas">DoseGridScaling</Run> so the stored values are
            in physical units.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Modality scope"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Only <Run FontFamily="Consolas">CT</Run>, <Run FontFamily="Consolas">MR</Run>, and
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">PT</Run> image series are linked to RT-STRUCT and RT-DOSE
            files during the scan. Other modalities (US, NM, etc.) appear in the tree and can be
            exported as <Run FontFamily="Consolas">image.nii.gz</Run>, but no structures or dose
            will be associated with them.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Multi-RT-STRUCT / multi-RT-DOSE per series"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            When several RT-STRUCT (or RT-DOSE) files reference the same image series, the scan links
            them all — the Series inspector counts and lists them — but Convert Selected exports only
            the first of each. If you need to export multiple structure sets from one image series,
            run the export twice with the unwanted RT-STRUCT files moved out of the input tree
            between runs.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Export Images OFF + Export Structures ON"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Masks are still rasterized to the image grid (or to the target spacing, if
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Resample to fixed spacing</Run> is ON), but no
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">image.nii.gz</Run> is written. The CSV manifest still records
            the series' voxel spacing and per-ROI volumes.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Limit export to selected ROIs"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            When ON: only ROIs whose DICOM name matches a defined association (or that you tick in
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Select ROIs for Export...</Run>) are exported, and matching
            ROIs are renamed to their canonical names. Patients whose series contain none of the
            selected ROIs are auto-deselected in the tree.
            <LineBreak/>
            When OFF: all ROIs are exported. Matched ROIs are still renamed to their canonical
            names; unmatched ROIs keep their original DICOM names.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Edit ROI Associations..."/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Define alias sets that all map to one canonical name. Persisted to
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">%AppData%\DicomToNifti\roi_associations.json</Run>.
            Example: canonical <Run FontFamily="Consolas">Target</Run> with aliases
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">CTV</Run>, <Run FontFamily="Consolas">GTV</Run>,
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">PTV</Run> — any DICOM ROI matching one of those aliases is
            exported as <Run FontFamily="Consolas">Target.nii.gz</Run>.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Select ROIs for Export..."/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Enabled only when <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Limit export to selected ROIs</Run> is ON.
            Lists every canonical name from the associations plus every unique DICOM ROI name
            discovered in the scan; tick the subset you want to export. Patients whose series have
            no matching ROI are auto-deselected when you click OK.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Resample to fixed spacing + Set Spacing..."/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            When the checkbox is ON, all exported volumes (image, dose, masks) are resampled to a
            uniform voxel spacing in mm. Click <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Set Spacing...</Run> to
            set X / Y / Z spacing. Image and dose use <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">linear</Run>
            interpolation; structure masks use <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">nearest-neighbour</Run> so
            labels stay binary. The target spacing is also recorded in the CSV manifest.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Anonymize export + Edit Anonymization Key..."/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            When ON, patient/series folder names are replaced with deterministic per-identifier hashes.
            Each identifier (MRN, Study UID, Series UID) is salted (default salt
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">"DicomToNifti"</Run>, configurable in Settings) and SHA256-hashed,
            and the resulting hash is used in place of the original identifier.
            <LineBreak/>
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">AnonymizationKey.json</Run> is written outside the export, by
            default to a folder beside it (<Run FontFamily="Consolas">{Output}.key\</Run>), and reused
            across sessions: the same identifier will always receive the same hash provided the file is
            preserved. <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Key location</Run> places it elsewhere (a file, or a
            folder to hold it); a location inside the export is refused, and the line under the field
            shows where the key will go. The log names the key's path at the end of every anonymized export.
            <LineBreak/>
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Edit Anonymization Key...</Run> opens that key file in an
            editor (or, with no output folder set, the Key location, else the
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">%AppData%\DicomToNifti\</Run> fallback) where you can view and
            override any input → hash mapping.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h1" Text="Reference: bottom action bar"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            The bar across the bottom of the window groups everything that drives an export. A
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Data to export</Run> row (the Export Images / Export Structures /
            Export Dose checkboxes plus the <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Export Options...</Run> button)
            sits directly above the run actions —
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Convert Selected</Run>,
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Export Manifest Only</Run>, and
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Settings...</Run> — with the timestamped log box beneath. See
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Reference: export options</Run> above for what each checkbox and
            the Export Options window produce.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Convert Selected"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Runs the full export for every selected series with the current Export Options applied.
            Writes <Run FontFamily="Consolas">image.nii.gz</Run>,
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">doses/{SeriesDescription}.nii.gz</Run>, and per-ROI
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">masks/*.nii.gz</Run> per series, plus
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">export_manifest.csv</Run> at the output root summarizing
            every exported series and its per-ROI volumes.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Export Manifest Only"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Inventory pass — writes only the CSV manifest
            (<Run FontFamily="Consolas">export_manifest_meta.csv</Run> at the output root). No
            .nii.gz files are produced; the rasterizer still runs in-memory to compute per-ROI
            volumes if <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Export Structures</Run> is ON. It logs the same
            non-uniform slice-spacing and missing-position warnings as Convert Selected.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Settings..."/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Per-app preferences, persisted to
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">%AppData%\DicomToNifti\settings.json</Run>:
            <LineBreak/>
            • <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Default Output Directory</Run> — pre-fills the Output field
              until you have used another output folder; from then on the last one used is restored
              (with the last Input folder) from
              <Run FontFamily="Consolas">%AppData%\DicomToNifti\gui_state.json</Run>.
            <LineBreak/>
            • <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Auto-open after conversion</Run> — opens the Output folder
              in the system file browser when Convert Selected finishes.
            <LineBreak/>
            • <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Anonymization salt</Run> (<Run FontFamily="Consolas">HashSalt</Run>) — stored in <Run FontFamily="Consolas">settings.json</Run> (not shown in this dialog); feeds the SHA256 hash for the per-identifier hashes. Changing the salt produces a different hash
              space; keep it stable across sessions to keep hashes reproducible.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h1" Text="Output structure"/>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Non-anonymized"/>
          <Border Background="{DynamicResource AppWindowBg}" BorderBrush="{DynamicResource AppBorder}" BorderThickness="1"
                  CornerRadius="2" Padding="10,8" Margin="0,4,0,4">
            <TextBlock FontFamily="Consolas" FontSize="11" Foreground="{DynamicResource AppHeadingText}" xml:space="preserve">{Output}/
│
├─ {PatientID}/
│   └─ {SeriesDate}_{SeriesDescription}/
│       ├─ image.nii.gz            → CT/MR/PT volume        <Run Foreground="{DynamicResource AppBadgeText}">(if Export Images is ON)</Run>
│       ├─ doses/
│       │   └─ {SeriesDescription}.nii.gz → RT-DOSE volume  <Run Foreground="{DynamicResource AppBadgeText}">(if Export Dose is ON and a dose is linked)</Run>
│       └─ masks/
│           └─ {ROI_Name}.nii.gz   → per-ROI binary mask    <Run Foreground="{DynamicResource AppBadgeText}">(if Export Structures is ON)</Run>
│
└─ export_manifest.csv                                      <Run Foreground="{DynamicResource AppBadgeText}">(or export_manifest_meta.csv from Export Manifest Only)</Run></TextBlock>
          </Border>

          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Anonymized"/>
          <Border Background="{DynamicResource AppWindowBg}" BorderBrush="{DynamicResource AppBorder}" BorderThickness="1"
                  CornerRadius="2" Padding="10,8" Margin="0,4,0,4">
            <TextBlock FontFamily="Consolas" FontSize="11" Foreground="{DynamicResource AppHeadingText}" xml:space="preserve">{Output}/
│
├─ {PatientHash}/{StudyHash}/{SeriesHash}/                  <Run Foreground="{DynamicResource AppBadgeText}">(hashed identifiers in place of {PatientID}/{Date}_...)</Run>
│   ├─ image.nii.gz                → CT/MR/PT volume
│   ├─ doses/
│   │   └─ {SeriesDescription}.nii.gz → RT-DOSE volume
│   └─ masks/
│       └─ {ROI_Name}.nii.gz       → per-ROI binary mask
│
└─ export_manifest.csv

{Output}.key/                                               <Run Foreground="{DynamicResource AppBadgeText}">(beside the export, not in it; or the Key location)</Run>
└─ AnonymizationKey.json                                    <Run Foreground="{DynamicResource AppBadgeText}">(deterministic identifier ↔ hash mapping)</Run></TextBlock>
          </Border>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Export Manifest Only</Run> writes
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">export_manifest_meta.csv</Run> at the output root instead of
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">export_manifest.csv</Run>; no per-series subfolders are created.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h1" Text="CSV manifest"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Two filenames depending on which button you clicked:
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">export_manifest.csv</Run> (Convert Selected) or
            <Run FontFamily="Consolas">export_manifest_meta.csv</Run> (Export Manifest Only). Same column
            format in both, written to the output root. If the file already exists, rows are merged
            by their (PatientID, StudyUID, SeriesUID) key and ROI columns are unioned — re-exporting
            updates and extends the manifest rather than overwriting it.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="Columns (in order)"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            • <Run FontFamily="Consolas">PatientID</Run> — DICOM PatientID (or its hash when anonymized), quoted.
            <LineBreak/>
            • <Run FontFamily="Consolas">StudyUID</Run> — DICOM StudyInstanceUID (or its hash), quoted.
            <LineBreak/>
            • <Run FontFamily="Consolas">SeriesUID</Run> — DICOM SeriesInstanceUID (or its hash), quoted.
            <LineBreak/>
            • <Run FontFamily="Consolas">SpacingX</Run>, <Run FontFamily="Consolas">SpacingY</Run>,
              <Run FontFamily="Consolas">SpacingZ</Run> — voxel spacing in mm. When
              <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Resample to fixed spacing</Run> is ON, these reflect the target
              spacing.
            <LineBreak/>
            • One column per unique canonical ROI name across all exported series — the value is the
              mask volume in cc, or <Run FontFamily="Consolas">-1</Run> if the row's series did not
              contain that ROI.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h1" Text="AnonymizationKey.json"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Written whenever <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Anonymize export</Run> was ON, outside the
            export: to <Run FontFamily="Consolas">{Output}.key\AnonymizationKey.json</Run> beside it by
            default, or to the <Run FontWeight="SemiBold">Key location</Run> in Export Options. It holds
            the salt plus three reverse-lookup maps — MRN → patient hash, Study UID → study hash,
            Series UID → series hash — so each original identifier always resolves to the same hash and
            the mapping can be inverted for de-anonymization. It is re-identification data: keep it,
            access-controlled, and do not share it with the export.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Determinism guarantee: identical identifier + identical salt always yields the same hash
            across sessions, provided the existing key file is preserved. Deleting it does not change
            the hashes (they are recomputed deterministically) but discards any manual overrides set in
            the key editor.
          </TextBlock>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            Exports made before the key moved out wrote it inside the output folder. The next anonymized
            export to that folder carries its mappings, overrides included, into the new location and
            logs a warning naming the old copy; move or delete that copy before sharing the export. It
            is never deleted for you.
          </TextBlock>

          <TextBlock Classes="h1" Text="Tips"/>
          <TextBlock Classes="body">
            • The log box at the bottom keeps a timestamped record of every step; the status bar at
              the very bottom of the window mirrors the latest message.
            <LineBreak/>
            • Closing this window returns you to the launcher. The Input and Output folders and every
              export checkbox are remembered across launches (they are saved as you change them); the
              scan results are not, so the next window starts with an empty tree.
            <LineBreak/>
            • The app follows the operating system's light or dark theme.
            <LineBreak/>
            • For very large archives, scan a subset first — Scan opens
              <Run FontStyle="Italic">every</Run> file in the input tree.
            <LineBreak/>
            • If the scan log reports "Skipped N file(s) due to errors" with messages about long
              paths, enable long-path support on the source drive — Windows refuses to open paths
              over 260 characters by default.
          </TextBlock>
        </StackPanel>
      </ScrollViewer>
    </Border>

    <Border Grid.Row="2" Background="{DynamicResource AppToolbarBg}" BorderBrush="{DynamicResource AppBorderAlt}"
            BorderThickness="0,1,0,0" Padding="12,8" Margin="0,12,0,0">
      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Right">
        <Button Content="Close" Click="CloseButton_Click" Width="90" Padding="12,6" IsCancel="True"/>
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>
  </Grid>
</Window>
